Teeno loop baar-baar kaam karte hain, farak **shart check hone ke samay** me hai:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    // for: ginti pata ho to sabse saaf
    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++)
        printf("for: %d\n", i);

    // while: shart pehle, kaam baad me
    int j = 1;
    while (j <= 3) {
        printf("while: %d\n", j);
        j++;
    }

    // do-while: kaam pehle, shart baad me (kam se kam EK baar chalega)
    int k = 1;
    do {
        printf("do-while: %d\n", k);
        k++;
    } while (k <= 3);
    return 0;
}
```

- **for** — jab pehle se pata ho kitni baar chalega (1 se 100 tak)
- **while** — jab shart par nirbhar ho (sahi input aane tak)
- **do-while** — jab kam se kam ek baar chalana hi ho (menu dikhana)

Sabse khaas baat: do-while me shart aakhir me check hoti hai, isiliye shart jhoothi ho tab bhi loop ek baar chal jata hai.

**break** loop ko wahi **tod** deta hai, **continue** us chance ko **chhod** kar agli baari par bhej deta hai:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
        if (i == 5)
            continue;          // 5 ko chhod do, aage badho
        if (i == 8)
            break;             // 8 par pura loop band
        printf("%d ", i);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Output: `1 2 3 4 6 7`

- i = 5 par continue hua — 5 print nahi hua, loop chala raha
- i = 8 par break hua — 8, 9, 10 kuch nahi aaya, loop khatm

Infinite loop wo loop hai jo **kabhi khatm hi nahi hota** — program wahi atka rehta hai.

Aam wajah — loop badhane wala kadam bhool jana:

```c
int i = 1;
while (i <= 10) {
    printf("%d ", i);
    // i++;  <-- ye bhool gaye! i hamesha 1 rahega, shart hamesha sahi
}
```

Ya jaan-boojh kar bhi banaya jata hai: `while (1) { ... }` — par andar sahi samay par `break` lagana padta hai.

Atke hue program ko rokne ke liye **Ctrl + C** dabao. Loop likhte hi ye check karo: andar koi na koi cheez badal rahi hai jo shart ko kabhi jhootha karegi?

