Ye dono pointer ki jaan hain, aur ek dusre ke **ulte** hain:

- `&` — **pata nikalo**: `&number` = number kahan rakha hai
- `*` — **pate par jao**: `*p` = us pate par rakhi value

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int number = 40;
    int *p = &number;        // & se pata pointer me rakha
    printf("Pata: %p\n", (void *)p);
    printf("*p = %d\n", *p); // * se pate wali value mili
    *p = 99;                 // * se value badal bhi di
    printf("Ab number = %d", number);
    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
Pata: (har baar alag number jaisa 0x7ffd...)
*p = 40
Ab number = 99
```

Dhyan do: pate wala number har run me alag ho sakta hai — asal baat value ki hai, pate ke number ki nahi.

Pointer se asal value badalna:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int number = 10;
    int *p = &number;
    printf("Pehle: %d\n", number);
    *p = 50;                      // pate par jaakar value badli
    printf("Pointer se badalne ke baad: %d", number);
    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
Pehle: 10
Pointer se badalne ke baad: 50
```

`number = 50` nahi likha, phir bhi number badal gaya — kyunki `*p` ka matlab hi hai 'number wali jagah'. Yahi pointer ki taakat hai, aur yahi wajah hai ki function bhi pointer se asal value badal sakta hai.

Function aur pointer ka sabse mashhoor program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp;
}

int main() {
    int x = 10, y = 20;
    printf("Pehle: x = %d, y = %d\n", x, y);
    swap(&x, &y);              // pate bheje, copy nahi
    printf("Baad me: x = %d, y = %d", x, y);
    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
Pehle: x = 10, y = 20
Baad me: x = 20, y = 10
```

- main ne x, y ke **pate** bheje — function ne un pato par jaakar value badal di
- Bina pointer ke ye swap sirf copy par hota aur asal x, y waisi hi rehti (call by value wali dikkat)
- Ise **call by reference** jaisa vyavhaar kehte hain — C me ye pointer se hi milta hai

**Call by value** — function ko value ki **nakal (copy)** milti hai, asal nahi badalta.
**Pointer se bhejna** — function ko **pata** milta hai, asal badal jata hai.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void copySe(int x) { x = 100; }        // copy badli
void pateSe(int *x) { *x = 100; }      // asal badla

int main() {
    int a = 10, b = 10;
    copySe(a);
    pateSe(&b);
    printf("Copy wala a: %d\n", a);    // 10 hi raha
    printf("Pate wala b: %d", b);      // 100 ho gaya
    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
Copy wala a: 10
Pate wala b: 100
```

C me asal me sab call by value hi hai — pointer bhejne par pate ki copy jati hai, par pata wahi rehta hai, isiliye asal value badal jati hai. Function se **ek se zyada value** badalni ho to pointer hi ekmatra rasta hai.

Array ka naam khud uske **pehle dabbe ka pata** hota hai — isiliye dono ka gehra rishta hai:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30};
    printf("%d\n", *arr);          // pehla dabba: 10
    printf("%d\n", *(arr + 1));    // agla dabba: 20
    printf("%d\n", *(arr + 2));    // teesra: 30
    printf("%d", arr[2]);          // wahi 30, aam tareeke se
    return 0;
}
```

- `arr[i]` asal me `*(arr + i)` ka hi chhota roop hai
- Pointer me 1 jodna matlab **agle dabbe** par jana (1 byte aage nahi — poore ek int aage)
- Isi liye function me array bhejne par uska pata jata hai, aur function asal array badal sakta hai

NULL pointer matlab — pointer **kahin nahi** ishara kar raha, usme koi sahi pata nahi:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int *p = NULL;              // abhi koi pata nahi diya
    if (p == NULL)
        printf("Pointer khaali hai, istemal mat karo\n");
    int number = 5;
    p = &number;                // ab sahi pata mil gaya
    printf("Ab value: %d", *p);
    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
Pointer khaali hai, istemal mat karo
Ab value: 5
```

- Khaali pointer par `*p` karna matlab bina pate ke ghar me ghusna — program **crash** ho jata hai
- Aadat: pointer banate hi ya to sahi pata do, ya NULL rakho — aur istemal se pehle check karo

Pointer ko badha-badha kar pure array ki sair:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int arr[] = {5, 10, 15, 20};
    int *p = arr;                    // p pehle dabbe par
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        printf("%d ", *p);
        p++;                         // agle dabbe par
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Output: `5 10 15 20`

`p++` karne se pointer 1 dabba aage sarak jata hai — int ke liye 4 byte aage, par hum 1 dabba gin te hain. Array aur pointer ka yehi khel bade programo me har jagah hai.

Pointer ka bhi ek pata hota hai — use rakhne wala pointer hi **pointer to pointer** hai:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int number = 30;
    int *p = &number;       // p me number ka pata
    int **pp = &p;          // pp me p ka pata
    printf("number = %d\n", number);
    printf("*p = %d\n", *p);
    printf("**pp = %d", **pp);
    return 0;
}
```

Output: teeno jagah `30`

- `*p` — ek kadam: pate par number
- `**pp` — do kadam: pehle p tak, phir uske pate par number
- Shuruaat me itna samajhna kaafi hai; iska asal istemal bade programo me hota hai

Jis pointer me **koi sahi pata hi nahi** (wild) ya jiska dabba **mit chuka** hai (dangling), use chhuna program ke liye khatarnaak hai:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int *p;              // wild pointer: isme kachra pata hai
    // *p = 10;          // KHATRA! pata nahi ye pata kiska hai - crash ho sakta hai
    int number = 10;
    p = &number;         // pehle sahi pata do
    *p = 20;             // ab surakshit hai
    printf("%d", number);
    return 0;
}
```

Output: `20`

Bachne ke niyam:
- Pointer banate hi sahi pata do, ya `NULL` rakho
- Kachra pate wala pointer kabhi-kabhi chal bhi jata hai aur kabhi crash — ye sabse mushkil galti hoti hai pakadne me

Pointer me sirf **pata** rehta hai — value chhoti ho ya badi, pate ka size ek jaisa. 64-bit system par har pointer **8 byte** ka hota hai:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("int*    : %zu byte\n", sizeof(int *));
    printf("char*   : %zu byte\n", sizeof(char *));
    printf("double* : %zu byte", sizeof(double *));
    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
int*    : 8 byte
char*   : 8 byte
double* : 8 byte
```

- 32-bit system par yahi 4 byte hota tha — system ke pate ki lambai par nirbhar hai
- Pointer ka type (int*/char*) size ke liye nahi, isliye hota hai ki `*p` par kitne byte padhne hain aur `p+1` kitna aage jayega

Format specifier aur variable ka type **milna lazmi** hai — galat jodi par jawab kachra aayega ya program ajeeb chalega:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int n;
    scanf("%f", &n);   // GALAT! n int hai, %f float padhega
    printf("%d", n);   // kachra value aayegi
    return 0;
}
```

- scanf float ke 4 byte ko int wale dabbe me zabardasti bharega — bit ka khel bigad jayega
- printf me bhi yahi niyam: int ke liye %d, float ke liye %f, char ke liye %c, string ke liye %s
- Compiler hamesha ye galti nahi pakadta, isiliye apni aankh se jodi milana seekho

break na lagao to program **agle case me bhi ghus jata hai** — ise fall through kehte hain:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int din = 2;
    switch (din) {
        case 1: printf("Somvaar\n");
        case 2: printf("Mangalvaar\n");   // din 2 hai, yahan se shuru
        case 3: printf("Budhvaar\n");     // break nahi, ye bhi chal gaya!
        default: printf("Aakhir\n");      // ye bhi!
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
Mangalvaar
Budhvaar
Aakhir
```

Har case ke aakhir me `break;` lagana aadat bana lo — ye C ki sabse chhupi galtiyon me se hai.

Jo variable banaya par value nahi di, usme **kachra (garbage) value** hoti hai — memory me pehle se pada kuch bhi:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int n;                    // value nahi di
    printf("%d", n);          // kuch bhi aa sakta hai: 0, 32765, -984512...
    return 0;
}
```

- Har run me alag jawab aa sakta hai — isiliye ye galti pakadni sabse mushkil hoti hai
- Loop ke yog (sum) wale program me `yog = 0` likhna isiliye lazmi hai — warna kachre me jod hota rahega
- Niyam: variable banate hi pehli value do, khaas taur par yog/ginati walo ko 0

if ke andar `=` lagana matlab tulna nahi, **value rakhna** — aur rakhi hui value 0 na ho to shart hamesha 'sach' mani jati hai:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int n = 5;
    if (n = 0)          // n me 0 rakh diya! shart jhoothi, ye nahi chalega
        printf("Pehla\n");
    if (n = 7)          // n me 7 rakh diya, shart sach, ye chalega
        printf("Dusra chala, n ab %d hai", n);
    return 0;
}
```

Output: `Dusra chala, n ab 7 hai`

Dekho do nuksan hue — shart bhi galat aur n ki asal value bhi mit gayi. gcc is par warning deta hai ('suggest parentheses'), use nazarandaz mat karo.

Char ki tulna ek-hare uddharan `' '` se hoti hai. Galti se `" "` laga diya to tulna ek pate (address) se hogi — jawab hamesha galat:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    char ch = 'a';
    if (ch == 'a')    // SAHI: akshar se akshar
        printf("Ye sahi tulna hai\n");
    if (ch == "a")    // GALAT: "a" ek string hai, ch ek akshar
        printf("Ye kabhi sahi se nahi chalega");
    return 0;
}
```

Compiler is par warning dega ('comparison between pointer and integer'). Niyam: ek akshar = `'a'`, poora shabd = `"a"` — dono duniya alag hai.

Header file compiler ko batati hai ki printf/scanf jaise function **hain kya aur kaise chalte hain**. Na lagao to:

```c
// #include <stdio.h>  <-- ye bhool gaye

int main() {
    printf("Namaste");   // compiler puchega: ye printf hai kya?
    return 0;
}
```

- Naye compiler error/warning denge: 'implicit declaration of function printf'
- Purane zamane me kabhi-kabhi chal bhi jata tha, par aaj ke gcc me ye sahi nahi mana jata
- Jo function use karo, uski header yaad rakho: printf/scanf → stdio.h, sqrt/pow → math.h, strlen/strcpy → string.h

C case sensitive hai — `main` ka matlab sirf chhota `main` hai:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int Main() {          // GALAT! Main (bada M) dusra function hai
    printf("Namaste");
    return 0;
}
```

Is par link ke samay error aayegi: 'undefined reference to main' — kyunki computer ko asal `main` (program ka darwaza) mila hi nahi.

Yahi haal `Printf`, `Scanf`, `Int` ka hai — C ke saare shabd chhote aksharo me hi likhe jate hain.

Har khula `{` ek band `}` mangta hai — ek bhi idhar-udhar to compiler ulajh jata hai:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    if (5 > 3) {
        printf("Bada hai");
    // } <-- ye band brace bhool gaye
    return 0;
}
```

- Error aksar program ke **aakhir** me dikhti hai ('expected } at end of input'), jabki bhool beech me hui hoti hai
- Bachne ka tareeka: `{` likhte hi turant `}` likh do, phir beech me code bharo
- Code ko andar ki ore daba kar (indent) likho — kaunsa brace kiska hai saaf dikhega

Float/double number memory me **lagbhag** sahi bante hain, pure nahi — isiliye `==` se tulna aksar galat jawab deti hai:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    double x = 0.1 + 0.2;
    printf("%.20f\n", x);          // 0.30000000000000004441...
    if (x == 0.3)
        printf("Barabar");
    else
        printf("Barabar NAHI - float ki chaal");
    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
0.30000000000000004441
Barabar NAHI - float ki chaal
```

Hal: tulna me farak dekho — `if (x - 0.3 < 0.0001 aur > -0.0001)` jaisi chhoti seema rakho. Paiso ke hisaab me to float ki jagah paise ko **int (paise me)** rakhna sabse surakshit hai.

Circle ke area ka sutra: π × r × r

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    float r = 7;
    float area = 3.14159 * r * r;
    printf("Vritt (circle) ka kshetrafal = %.2f", area);
    return 0;
}
```

Output: `Vritt (circle) ka kshetrafal = 153.94`

- π ki value 3.14159 rakhi hai — chaho to `#define PI 3.14159` karke upar rakh sakte ho
- Jawab float me aata hai kyunki π dashamlav wala hai

Circle ke area ka sutra: π × r × r

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    float r = 5;
    float area = 3.14159 * r * r;
    printf("Vritt (circle) ka kshetrafal = %.2f", area);
    return 0;
}
```

Output: `Vritt (circle) ka kshetrafal = 78.54`

- π ki value 3.14159 rakhi hai — chaho to `#define PI 3.14159` karke upar rakh sakte ho
- Jawab float me aata hai kyunki π dashamlav wala hai

Aayat ke do sutra — area = lambai × chaudai, perimeter = 2 × (lambai + chaudai):

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int l = 10, b = 5;
    printf("Aayat ka kshetrafal = %d", l * b);
    printf("\nParimap (perimeter) = %d", 2 * (l + b));
    return 0;
}
